{{!-- HOMEFRONT — the GM playbook's Homefront page, whole. Reference only: nothing here rolls,
      nothing here is stored, and the tables come from module/gm-toolkit/homefront-text.js by way
      of homefront-view.js.

      The printed page is four columns of four different KINDS of thing, and this tab keeps all
      four rather than picking the tidiest: the village's own facts, the questions to ask about
      it, the year's work, the two procedures that bracket an expedition, the move a GM has to
      answer, and what a trade Value is worth. They are on one page in the book because they are
      what "we're at home now" needs, and splitting them across tabs would mean knowing which of
      the four you wanted before you looked.

      ORDER is the playbook's, read down each column and then across, and it happens to run from
      the most-used to the least: a GM frames a homefront scene far more often than they price a
      gemstone.

      `data-tab="homefront"` earns the rail's village glyph; the icon table in stonetop.css is one
      flat list keyed by data-tab with no per-sheet scope.

      Each section is BOXED in its own `.stonetop-move-group`, the same as the Moves tab's three
      lists and for the same reason: the fold walk claims a heading's FOLLOWING SIBLINGS until it
      meets the next heading (utils/section-editing.js), so seven headings in one flat run would
      let the first caret swallow the six below it.

      The `{{#if (eq kind ...)}}` chain is a switch over six body shapes, and it is spelled out
      rather than pushed into six more partial files because every branch is short and the whole
      of what this tab renders is then readable in one place. Aftermath and Downtime share the
      "steps" branch, which is the only reuse there is: their DATA is two separate tables (see
      homefront-view.js), so sharing the renderer cannot make one read as the other. --}}
<div class="tab homefront" data-group="primary" data-tab="homefront">
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-homefront">

		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.homefront.intro"}}</p>

		{{#each stonetop.homefrontSections}}
		<div class="stonetop-move-group stonetop-gm-homefront-section">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=title note=note collapse=collapseId}}

			{{!-- LIFE IN STONETOP. Four groups of facts, each citing its own Book II spread: the
			      four are four separate spreads there, and a GM chasing one of them wants the
			      right page rather than the entry's first. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "groups")}}
			<div class="stonetop-gm-homefront-groups">
				{{#each body.groups}}
				<section class="stonetop-gm-homefront-group">
					<h4 class="stonetop-gm-homefront-group-title">{{name}}</h4>
					<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items">
						{{#each items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
					</ul>
					<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-page">{{pageRef}}</p>
				</section>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- QUESTIONS TO ASK. Every one of these ends in "?", so every one is tagged
			      `question-bullet` by markQuestionBullets (hooks/RenderActorSheet.js) on the way
			      past. That is a bullet-picture swap only — see the note on the class in
			      stonetop.css — so the list lays out like any other here. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "questions")}}
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items stonetop-gm-homefront-questions">
				{{#each body.items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
			</ul>
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- THE YEAR'S WORK. Five blocks, and the one the campaign is actually in is marked.

			      The mark is a comparison against `stonetop.homefrontSeason`, which the sheet reads
			      off the steading's clock on each render — NOT a flag stamped onto the season by
			      the view-model, which is built once and frozen for the life of the page. `@root`
			      is needed because inside this `{{#each}}` the context is the season.

			      "Always" carries a key that is deliberately not a SEASON_IDS value, so it never
			      matches and is never the marked block. That is correct rather than a gap: its
			      chores are true in all four seasons, so marking it "now" would say nothing.

			      NO per-season class on these blocks. The obvious one to add would be
			      `--spring`/`--summer`/..., and the only thing it could usefully carry is that
			      season's ink — which belongs to the steading header's clock alone and to nothing
			      else (see the block in stonetop.css, and the test that counts every read of those
			      four tokens). A hook with no rules behind it is the door back. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "seasons")}}
			<div class="stonetop-gm-homefront-seasons">
				{{#each body.seasons}}
				<section class="stonetop-gm-homefront-season{{#if (eq key @root.stonetop.homefrontSeason)}} stonetop-gm-homefront-season--now{{/if}}">
					<h4 class="stonetop-gm-homefront-season-title">{{name}}{{#if (eq key @root.stonetop.homefrontSeason)}}
						<span class="stonetop-gm-homefront-now">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.homefront.now"}}</span>{{/if}}</h4>
					<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items">
						{{#each items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
					</ul>
				</section>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- AFTERMATH and DOWNTIME. One renderer, two tables.

			      Always an `<ol>`, with the numbering turned off by a class for the list that is
			      not a sequence — the same shape the Core Loop tab uses for its two charts, and
			      for the same reason: a Handlebars branch that picks the TAG NAME has to repeat
			      the whole list body inside both arms of it.

			      A step's `name` and its `detail` are one of the playbook's sentences with the
			      first clause bolded, so they are joined here by a single space and nothing else.
			      That is why several details begin in lower case. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "steps")}}
			<ol class="stonetop-gm-homefront-steps{{#unless body.numbered}} stonetop-gm-homefront-steps--unnumbered{{/unless}}">
				{{#each body.steps}}
				<li class="stonetop-gm-homefront-step">
					<strong class="stonetop-gm-homefront-step-name">{{name}}</strong>{{#if detail}} <span class="stonetop-gm-homefront-step-detail">{{detail}}</span>{{/if}}{{#if pageRef}} <span class="stonetop-gm-homefront-step-page">{{pageRef}}</span>{{/if}}
					{{#if items}}
					<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items">
						{{#each items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
					</ul>
					{{/if}}
				</li>
				{{/each}}
			</ol>
			{{#if body.ends}}<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-ends">{{body.ends}}</p>{{/if}}
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- MAKE A PLAN. The one PLAYER move on this page, and it is here because answering it
			      is a GM procedure: the requirements below are what the answer is built out of, and
			      the list means nothing without the trigger that summons it. So the move's own text
			      leads, set apart as the book's words, and the requirements follow. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "plan")}}
			<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-trigger">{{body.trigger}}</p>
			<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-guidance">{{body.guidance}}</p>
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items stonetop-gm-homefront-requirements">
				{{#each body.requirements}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
			</ul>
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- RELATIVE VALUE. Five tiers, and the notes under them do real work: the asterisk
			      answers the two entries that carry one, and the last is the reminder that what
			      is really being traded is barter, debts and honor rather than coin.

			      The LEAD is Book I's paragraph rather than the playbook's, for the reason
			      homefront-text.js gives: the playbook's condensation drops the one thing a table
			      most often gets wrong, which is that these are tiers rather than a scale. It sets
			      the whole table up, so it goes above it, in the book's voice. --}}
			{{#if (eq kind "value")}}
			<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-lead">{{body.lead}}</p>
			<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-intro">{{body.intro}}</p>
			<div class="stonetop-gm-homefront-tiers">
				{{#each body.tiers}}
				<section class="stonetop-gm-homefront-tier">
					<h4 class="stonetop-gm-homefront-tier-title">{{format "stonetop.gmToolkit.homefront.valueTier" value=value}}</h4>
					<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-items">
						{{#each items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
					</ul>
				</section>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-homefront-notes">
				{{#each body.notes}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
			</ul>
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- The section's page citation, ONCE, after the switch rather than at the end of
			      each of its branches. Only one branch renders, and every one of them ended with
			      the same line — so five copies said what one says, and a seventh section kind
			      would have needed a sixth. Guarded because Life in Stonetop is the one section
			      with no citation of its own: its four groups each cite their own spread, above. --}}
			{{#if body.pageRef}}<p class="stonetop-gm-homefront-page">{{body.pageRef}}</p>{{/if}}
		</div>
		{{/each}}

	</section>
</div>
